---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'How Slip & Fall Claims Work | slipfallcalculator.com';
const description = 'A step-by-step guide to the slip and fall claims process from incident documentation to settlement.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>How Slip &amp; Fall Claims Work</h1>
  <h2>Step 1: Document the Scene</h2>
  <p>Photograph the hazard before it's cleaned up. Get names of witnesses. Request an incident report from the property owner or manager and keep a copy.</p>
  <h2>Step 2: Seek Medical Attention</h2>
  <p>See a doctor immediately — even if you feel okay. Insurance companies use gaps in treatment to argue your injuries weren't serious. Keep all records and bills.</p>
  <h2>Step 3: Preserve Evidence</h2>
  <p>Send a letter to the property owner asking them to preserve security camera footage. Footage is often overwritten within 30–90 days.</p>
  <h2>Step 4: File an Insurance Claim</h2>
  <p>Notify the property owner's liability insurer. Do not give a recorded statement before consulting an attorney.</p>
  <h2>Step 5: Establish Liability</h2>
  <p>Your attorney investigates whether the owner had notice of the hazard and failed to address it. Key evidence: maintenance logs, prior incident reports, employee training records.</p>
  <h2>Step 6: Calculate Damages</h2>
  <p>Total all medical bills, future care estimates, lost wages, and non-economic damages. Our calculator provides a starting estimate.</p>
  <h2>Step 7: Negotiate or Litigate</h2>
  <p>Most cases settle before trial. Your attorney sends a demand letter; the insurer responds with a counter-offer. If negotiations stall, filing suit often prompts a more reasonable offer.</p>
</BaseLayout>
